{
 "lead": "A diode rectifier turns AC into pulsating DC using only the one-way conduction of a diode, so its average output is fixed by the supply peak and the circuit shape. Half-wave, centre-tap and bridge circuits are the three standard shapes, found at the front of almost every mains-powered supply. This chapter derives their average and RMS output, ripple factor, peak inverse voltage (PIV) and transformer use, and shows what an inductive load does.",
 "prereq": "You need: the power diode and its static characteristic (a conducting silicon diode drops about 0.7 V; a reverse-biased one blocks up to its rated reverse voltage), from the power semiconductor devices topic.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Half-wave rectifier with R and RL load",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "One diode connects the AC source vs = Vm sin θ to the load, where Vm is the peak supply voltage in volts and θ = ωt is the angle in radians. In the positive half-cycle the diode conducts and the load sees vs. In the negative half-cycle the diode blocks and the load voltage is zero. The output is one half-sine pulse per supply cycle, so the averages are taken over the full period 2π."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Vdc = (1/2π) ∫₀^π Vm sin θ dθ = Vm/π = 0.318 Vm",
     "where": "Vdc is the average (DC) output voltage in V."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Vrms = √[(1/2π) ∫₀^π Vm² sin²θ dθ] = Vm/2 = 0.5 Vm",
     "where": "Vrms is the RMS output voltage in V."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "FF = Vrms/Vdc = π/2 = 1.57;   RF = √(FF² − 1) = 1.21",
     "where": "FF is the form factor and RF the ripple factor, both plain numbers. The diode blocks the full negative peak, so PIV = Vm. The ripple repeats once per cycle, at the supply frequency f."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "With an <b>RL load</b> the inductor keeps current flowing after θ = π, so the diode stays on and the load follows vs into its negative half until the current reaches zero at the <b>extinction angle</b> β (between π and 2π). The negative part lowers the average: Vdc = (Vm/2π)(1 − cos β). A <b>freewheeling diode</b> across the load takes over the current at π, clamps the load voltage at zero and restores Vdc = Vm/π."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The transformer utilisation factor TUF = Pdc / (secondary VA rating) is only 0.287 here: the secondary works half the time and carries a DC component that pushes the core towards saturation."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Centre-tap full-wave rectifier",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A centre-tapped transformer has two equal halves, each giving Vm peak measured from the centre tap. Diode D1 conducts on the positive half-cycle and D2 on the negative one, and both push current through the load in the same direction. The output has two pulses per supply cycle, so the average is taken over π."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Vdc = (1/π) ∫₀^π Vm sin θ dθ = 2Vm/π = 0.637 Vm",
     "where": "Vm is the peak of each half of the secondary in V."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Vrms = Vm/√2 = 0.707 Vm;   FF = 1.11;   RF = 0.482",
     "where": "The ripple is at 2f, so 100 Hz on a 50 Hz supply."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "When D1 conducts, the load's positive end sits at +Vm above the centre tap while the anode of D2 is at the far end of the secondary, −Vm. So D2 blocks 2Vm: the PIV is the voltage across the whole secondary. Some syllabi call this a two-pulse (p = 2) rectifier, p being the output pulses per supply cycle. Its TUF is 0.57 for the secondary and 0.81 for the primary, about 0.69 on average."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Bridge rectifier and comparison",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Four diodes in a bridge give full-wave output without a centre tap. The AC supply connects to the two nodes where D1 meets D4 and D3 meets D2; the load connects across the other two nodes, which become DC+ and DC−. D1 and D2 conduct together in one half-cycle, D3 and D4 in the other, and the load current keeps the same direction."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Vdc = 2Vm/π;   Vrms = Vm/√2;   RF = 0.482;   PIV = Vm;   TUF ≈ 0.81",
     "where": "Vm is the peak supply voltage in V."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Each off diode is connected straight across the supply through a conducting diode, so it blocks the full Vm on its own; the two off diodes do not share the voltage. The price is two diode drops in the current path, about 1.4 V for silicon, which matters only at low output voltages. Replacing the diodes by thyristors gives phase control: see <a href=\"#single-phase-half-and-fully-controlled-rectifiers\">single-phase controlled rectifiers</a>."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Circuit",
      "Diodes",
      "Vdc",
      "PIV per diode",
      "Ripple",
      "TUF"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Half-wave",
       "1",
       "Vm/π = 0.318 Vm",
       "Vm",
       "f",
       "0.287"
      ],
      [
       "Centre-tap",
       "2",
       "2Vm/π = 0.637 Vm",
       "2Vm",
       "2f",
       "about 0.69"
      ],
      [
       "Bridge",
       "4",
       "2Vm/π = 0.637 Vm",
       "Vm",
       "2f",
       "0.81"
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A single-phase bridge rectifier feeds a 100 Ω resistive load from a 230 V rms, 50 Hz supply. Take the diodes as ideal. Find Vm, Vdc, Idc, Vrms, Irms, the form factor, the ripple factor, the PIV of each diode and the output ripple frequency.",
   "steps": [
    "Vm = √2 × 230 = 325.27 V.",
    "Vdc = 2Vm/π = 2 × 325.27 / 3.1416 = 207.07 V.",
    "Idc = Vdc/R = 207.07 / 100 = 2.07 A.",
    "Vrms = Vm/√2 = 325.27 / 1.4142 = 230.0 V. Irms = 230.0 / 100 = 2.30 A.",
    "FF = Vrms/Vdc = 230.0 / 207.07 = 1.111. RF = √(1.111² − 1) = √0.2337 = 0.483 (0.482 when FF is rounded to 1.11 first).",
    "PIV = Vm = 325.27 V.",
    "Ripple frequency = 2f = 2 × 50 = 100 Hz."
   ],
   "answer": "Vm = 325.27 V, Vdc = 207.07 V, Idc = 2.07 A, Vrms = 230.0 V, Irms = 2.30 A, FF = 1.111, RF = 0.483, PIV = 325.27 V and 100 Hz ripple."
  },
  {
   "question": "A half-wave diode rectifier on 230 V, 50 Hz feeds R = 10 Ω in series with L = 31.8 mH (ωL = 10 Ω). The current dies out at β = 225.8°. Find Vdc without and with a freewheeling diode.",
   "steps": [
    "Vm/2π = 325.27 / 6.2832 = 51.77 V.",
    "Without a freewheeling diode the output follows vs from 0 to β: Vdc = (Vm/2π)(1 − cos β) = 51.77 × (1 − cos 225.8°) = 51.77 × (1 + 0.6971) = 87.86 V.",
    "With a freewheeling diode the output is clamped at zero after π, leaving the plain half-sine: Vdc = Vm/π = 103.54 V, the same as for a pure 10 Ω load.",
    "Average load current with the freewheeling diode: Idc = Vdc/R = 103.54 / 10 = 10.35 A (the inductor has no average voltage, so only R sets Idc)."
   ],
   "answer": "87.86 V without the freewheeling diode, 103.54 V with it."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Averaging the half-wave output over π instead of 2π. That gives 2Vm/π, the full-wave value; the half-wave average is Vm/π = 0.318 Vm.",
  "Quoting the full-wave ripple factor 0.482 for a half-wave circuit, whose ripple factor is 1.21.",
  "Giving the centre-tap PIV as Vm. It is 2Vm, the voltage across the whole secondary; the bridge diode sees Vm.",
  "Using 230 V as Vm. For a 230 V rms supply, Vm = 230√2 = 325.3 V.",
  "Calling TUF an efficiency. It compares Pdc with the transformer's VA rating."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "What is the PIV of each diode in a bridge rectifier and in a centre-tap rectifier with peak Vm?",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "Bridge: Vm. Centre-tap: 2Vm (Vm here is the peak of each half of the secondary)."
  },
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "A full-wave rectifier runs from a 50 Hz supply. What is the output ripple frequency?",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "2f = 100 Hz, because there are two output pulses per supply cycle."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why is the PIV of a centre-tap rectifier diode 2Vm?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "When D1 conducts, the common cathode point sits at +Vm above the centre tap, while the anode of D2 is at the other end of the secondary, −Vm. D2 therefore blocks Vm − (−Vm) = 2Vm."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A half-wave rectifier uses a 230 V rms, 50 Hz supply and R = 50 Ω. Find Vm, Vdc, Idc, Vrms and PIV.",
   "marks": 5,
   "a": "Vm = 325.27 V. Vdc = Vm/π = 103.54 V. Idc = 103.54/50 = 2.07 A. Vrms = Vm/2 = 162.63 V. PIV = Vm = 325.27 V."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "A bridge and a centre-tap rectifier give the same Vdc from the same Vm. Compare them for a 600 V DC supply.",
   "marks": 5,
   "a": "Bridge: 4 diodes with PIV Vm, plain transformer, TUF 0.81, two diode drops. Centre-tap: 2 diodes with PIV 2Vm, centre-tapped transformer, TUF about 0.69, one drop. At 600 V the bridge wins; the centre-tap suits low-voltage, high-current supplies."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "What does a freewheeling diode change in a half-wave rectifier with RL load, and why does Vdc rise?",
   "marks": 6,
   "a": "At θ = π it takes over the inductor current, so the load voltage is clamped at zero instead of following the negative supply until β. The negative area disappears, Vdc rises from (Vm/2π)(1 − cos β) to Vm/π, and the load current flows all the time."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. Real bridge modules are rated by average current, surge current and reverse voltage. Their conduction loss is about 2 × VF × Idc, 14 W at 10 A, which is why low-voltage supplies use synchronous rectifiers (MOSFETs switched as diodes). Search for 'synchronous rectification' and 'inrush current limiting' next.",
